      <span class="section-label">Overview</span>
      <h2 class="content-title">About Multiple Viewpoints Trek</h2>


  <div class="title-divider"></div>

  <div class="about-text">
        <p><strong>The Multiple Viewpoints Trek is a 14-day journey from Pokhara that links six of the Annapurna region's best viewpoints in one route</strong>: the Mardi Himal Viewpoint, Muldai, Khopra Ridge, the sacred Khayer Lake at 4,600 m, Poon Hill and Kokhe Danda, the highest point of Parbat district. Each one shows the Annapurna and Dhaulagiri ranges from a different angle, and the trail between them blends popular highlights with quiet, community-run sections that most trekkers never see.</p>
        <p>The trek begins with a short drive from Pokhara to Kande and follows the Mardi Himal route, climbing through rhododendron and oak forest onto high ridges with striking views of Machhapuchhre and Annapurna South. This section gives a close-up Himalayan experience, with constantly changing scenery and dramatic ridgeline walking that sets the tone for the journey ahead.</p>
        <p>From the Mardi region, the route descends to the Modi Khola and climbs to Ghandruk, a large Gurung village with stone houses, flagstone lanes and spectacular mountain backdrops. Moving away from the busier trails, the trek continues through Tadapani to Dobato and the Muldai Viewpoint, one of the finest sunrise spots in the region, with wide panoramas across Annapurna and Dhaulagiri.</p>
        <p>As the journey continues to Khopra Ridge, the landscape opens into alpine pasture and high ridges with a completely different angle on the mountains. This section is notably quiet, with the community lodge on the ridge looking straight across the Kali Gandaki gorge to Dhaulagiri, while Annapurna South, Nilgiri and the surrounding peaks fill the skyline.</p>
        <p>A highlight of the trek is the full-day hike from Khopra to the sacred Khayer Lake, a high-altitude lake beneath Annapurna South. It is the toughest day of the route and the most remote, adding both spiritual and visual depth to the experience and showing the raw, untouched beauty of the high Himalayas.</p>
        <p>The trail then descends to Swanta and climbs to Ghorepani for Poon Hill. Instead of joining the pre-dawn crowd, we climb after breakfast, when the viewpoint is almost empty. Unlike typical itineraries, the journey continues beyond Poon Hill into Parbat district and Kokhe Danda, a hidden gem with wide, peaceful panoramic views far from the crowds.</p>
        <p>The final section through Kokhe Danda and Lespar stands out for its authenticity, passing through quiet Magar villages, terraced fields and untouched trails, with a last night in a family homestay and a momo-making session with your hosts. On the final day we walk through Haljure to Banau and drive back to Pokhara via Kushma.</p>
        <p>The Multiple Viewpoints Trek is ideal for trekkers seeking a longer, more varied adventure that combines famous highlights with hidden routes, delivering multiple perspectives of the Annapurna and Dhaulagiri ranges along with rich Gurung and Magar culture.</p>
      </div>

